  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWest to northwest flow aloft continues over the Northern Plains,\nwith several weak shortwaves moving through our area on top of \nthe south central CONUS ridge. Model performance of the timing\nof these small features has been poor, but one shortwave coming\nup through the Rockies could bring some showers and\nthunderstorms this evening. Upper heights build up a bit\ntomorrow and most model solutions keep our CWA dry, but can't\ncompletely rule out another weak ridge rider. A bit better\nsignal for a shortwave coming through Friday night into\nSaturday, and the NBM has the highest probabilities for\nprecipitation over 0.10\" during this time period, mainly along\nand north of I-94. After that shortwave moves by and high\npressure brings dry conditions for Sunday, the west to\nnorthwesterly flow aloft continues. Minor shortwaves that could\nbring some precipitation is not resolvable at this point, but\ntemps look fairly seasonable through the first part of next\nweek.\n\n...Thunderstorm chances and severe potential tonight...\n\nThe upper forcing is still well off to our southwest, but with\nML CAPE around 2500 J/kg in south central ND some convective\ntowers have already started to develop. Many of the CAMs bring\nmost of the activity into southeastern ND later this evening\nwell after 00Z as thunderstorms come east off the High Plains.\nBut as stated above the CAMs have not been doing well lately.\nAny showers and thunderstorms that do develop have severe\npotential with deep layer shear around 50 kts, but there is \nalso some displacement from the main forcing. HREF has the best\nprobabilities of strong updraft helicity values mostly in SD\nwith just some 10 to 20 percent chances in our far southwestern\ncounties. Machine learning models also have severe chances\naround 5 to 15 percent in portions of our southern counties.\nWill continue to keep an eye on how storms develop, but for now\nthe marginal risk for hail up to quarters and 60 mph seems\nreasonable.",
